{"name":"Jaunty Green","hex":"#8dc36a","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"jaunty","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Airy; showy; finical.","Characterized by an affected or fantastical manner."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A master-at-arms on board a Royal Navy ship."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/jaunty"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ubuntu version history","blocks":[{"text":"Releases of Ubuntu—a Linux operating system mostly composed of free and open-source software—are made semiannually by Canonical Ltd using the year and month of the release as a version number. The first Ubuntu release, for example, was Ubuntu 4.10 and was released on 20 October 2004. Consequently, version numbers for future versions are provisional; if the release is delayed until a different month than planned, the version number will change accordingly."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Ubuntu_26.04_LTS_desktop.png/330px-Ubuntu_26.04_LTS_desktop.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ubuntu_version_history"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Ubuntu-desktop-2-410-20080706.png/500px-Ubuntu-desktop-2-410-20080706.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Ubuntu-desktop-2-410-20080706.png/500px-Ubuntu-desktop-2-410-20080706.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Ubuntu 4.10 (Warty Warthog)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AUbuntu-desktop-2-410-20080706.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Ubuntu-desktop-2-504-20080706.png/500px-Ubuntu-desktop-2-504-20080706.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Ubuntu-desktop-2-504-20080706.png/500px-Ubuntu-desktop-2-504-20080706.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Ubuntu 5.04 (Hoary Hedgehog)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AUbuntu-desktop-2-504-20080706.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Ubuntu-desktop-2-510-20080706.png/500px-Ubuntu-desktop-2-510-20080706.png?utm_sou